================================================================================
DalaShift — LICENSE NOT YET SELECTED
================================================================================

NO OPEN-SOURCE LICENSE HAS BEEN APPROVED FOR THIS PROJECT YET.

This file is a placeholder, NOT a license. Until the team records an approved
license in this file, no grant of rights is made to copy, distribute, modify
or sublicense the contents of this repository.

STATUS: OPEN — awaiting team decision.
TRACKED IN: docs/decisions/ADR-0001-open-source-license.md
DECISION OWNER: all three team members (unanimous)
DEADLINE: before the NASA Space Apps submission is finalised.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY THIS IS DELIBERATELY UNRESOLVED
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Choosing a license is a legal and strategic decision that must be made by the
team, not by a tool. It determines:

  - Whether the code may be reused, modified and redistributed by others.
  - Whether the project can satisfy the NASA Space Apps open-source
    participation requirement (verify against the official rules — see
    docs/hackathon/README.md).
  - Whether third-party datasets and APIs embedded in the project impose
    additional obligations (see docs/api/data-sources.md).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PENDING DECISION — the team must choose exactly one
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Option A — MIT / Apache-2.0
      Permissive, the most common choice for hackathon and community projects.
      Maximum adoption. Requires the Apache-2.0 patent grant if Apache-2.0 is
      chosen.

  Option B — AGPL-3.0
      Strong copyleft. Any derivative deployed as a network service must
      release its source. Prevents a closed hosted fork of the work.

  Option C — GPL-3.0
      Strong copyleft for distributed derivatives, with a network-use
      carve-out.

  Option D — A dedicated or restricted license
      For example, a "source-available" license, or one that restricts use by
      commercial agritech competitors. Requires legal review before use.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
DATA AND DATASET LICENSING IS SEPARATE FROM CODE LICENSING
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The software license does NOT grant rights to the data it consumes. Each
external source has its own terms — NASA data is generally open under the
NASA Open Data / Earth Science Data policies, ISRIC SoilGrids is CC BY 4.0,
and basemap tiles carry provider-specific attribution and usage conditions
(see docs/api/data-sources.md). Any license chosen for the code must be
accompanied by the correct attribution and usage compliance for each dataset.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
HOW TO RESOLVE THIS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  1. Discuss and agree the option as a team (unanimous).
  2. Record the decision in docs/decisions/ADR-0001-open-source-license.md.
  3. Replace THIS file with the full, unmodified, official text of the chosen
     license.
  4. Update the "License" section of README.md.
  5. Add the SPDX identifier to each package.json ("license" field).
  6. Re-tag the release commit.

Until step 3 is complete, treat the repository as UNLICENSED and do not
redistribute it or submit it as an open-source project.
